A||||18||Mach10||Increased Range Part II -- The Sequel||||8||
Z||000000||Mach10||06-24-2001||03:18 AM||||Well, I reset the codes, and after about 10kms of truly bizarre engine behavior, everything smoothed out, and the car felt great. That is, until I let the damn thing sit idling for 5 minutes. Our favorite "Yes, I'm still alive" light comes on, AFTER I had reset the damn thing. So I pull the codes, and get a 44, which according to haynes, means "Lean Exhaust." I went to the back and sure enough, stinky. So I'm fairly confident I'm running rich. No black smoke, but it smells acrid. <P>I'd be thinking that my 02 sensor is shot, since I have all the usual "rich" signs: Sooty exhaust, popping when coasting in 1-low, smelly exhaust. Am I on the right track? I'm taking the smell test with a grain of salt seeing as I cut the cat out and had a resonator welded in...||||reg||8
Z||000001||acehilton||06-24-2001||07:51 AM||||I had this same problem with my Ford pickup a year or so ago.  The exhaust was sooted up, you could smell the cat at all times, and the gas mileage went in the toilet.  I ran the self test and I got a code for the O2 sensor sending a continues lean signal.  The ECMs won't allow the engine to get lean enough to hurt itself, so it goes into survival mode of running richer then it should (starts running open loop versus closed loop).  <P>Anyway, I replaced the O2 sensor, and problem solved.  All I had to do then was replace the burnt up cat (to much rich a mixture for to long, burnt it up) and I was back to normal.  <P>About 6 months later, the same thing happened to my 92 SHO.  This time I changed the sensors at the first sighn of trouble.  Again, problem solved.  <P>So I would try swaping in a new O2 sensor.  Hope this helps.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-24-2001||12:08 PM||||Could be the O2 easy enough.<P>double check the exhaust for something loose or cracked as well. that can let air into the exhaust which makes the O2 sensor think it's lean and then the ECM run it rich.<P>You may also have a failed catalyst now. running rich can make them overheat and die.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||06-24-2001||01:10 PM||||Curiosity killed my cat. Curiosity is my mechanic...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I had the thing tossed a while ago. I guess I'll change the 02 sensor next. Anyone recommend a good anti-seize compound?||||reg||6
Z||000004||acehilton||06-24-2001||01:41 PM||||All of the 02 sensors that I have installed have come with the stuff allready on the threads.<P>I have always had luck with the anti-sieze made by Permatex (it's sold in a small gray bottle).<P>A word of experience, use a little Knocker' Loose on the old 02 sensor befor you try to take it out of the manifold.  This is definitely a knuckle saver.||||reg||1
Z||000005||baldlobo||06-24-2001||02:01 PM||||I'd say it is the O2 sensor but if the lean code pops up again replace the exhaust gaskets(V6 right?).||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mach10||06-24-2001||02:17 PM||||Someone I know suggested that it *could* be the MAP sensor... (Or was is MAS? I don't think the MPI system has one)<BR>Thoughts? I'm ordering the part today.||||reg||14
Z||000007||theogre||06-24-2001||03:22 PM||||There is an air temp sensor in the intake. in/near the air cleaner on V6. If that sensor is out of calibration then the ECM can do odd things. On V6, it's usually called an "air charge temp" sensor. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||06-24-2001||04:08 PM||||I'll switch out the O2 first, before flocking around with the rest of the EFI... Keep you posted, Ogre-dude. You are the Man(Biped?)||||reg||6
Z||000009||Ken Wittlief||06-24-2001||05:42 PM||||the MAP sensor will make the car run rich if it is bad - or if its vacuum line is leaking. For a quick test pull the MAP sensor line off the intake plenium and try sucking on it - put a short piece of clean vacuum line on it - it should stick to your tongue and stay there. if not trace the vacuum line out - it dissapears under the center of the plenium and you will need a dental mirror and flashlite to see the far end when it attaches to the fuel pressure valve.<P>  But it sounds like your O2 is bad - they really do need to be replaced every 20 to 30k miles - and when they go bad they read lean, and tell the ECM to put out more fuel - and the car runs stinky rich - and your mileage goes down the tubes.<P>  Its not hard to replace the O2 - you need to run the car a few minutes to get the pipe hot - then take it off - If you pull the connector off behind the engine first you can get a box wrench over it. Easy to burn yourself though.<P>  also when you replace it reset the ECM again so it will calibrate to the new sensor.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-24-2001||06:05 PM||||Say Ogre<P>  you have to explain to me?! How do you know what your MPG performance is if you dont top the tank off until it gets to the filler neck? how do you know if its a gallon from the top, or two, or a quart, or an ounce?!<P>  and how do you know how full it was last time?!<P>  I expect the point at which the handle kicks off the first time, the second... is very un-repeatable - so maybe you were getting better gas mileage when you didnt top it off cause you didnt really know if you were putting as much fuel in the tank as you did last time. I dont think the gas gauge is a good indicator either.<P> I have noticed when I fill the tank the gauge will point near full after I start the car - and during the next mile or so it will climb - I doubt its accurate near the top.<P>  You are right about getting better mileage with the auto sometimes. I use to drive my 5speed saturn around in 4th gear all the time under 45. Then I noticed the auto in the fiero kicks right up to 3rd and locks the TC quickly with a light foot on the pedal, and it drops the engine down to 1500 rpm in town.<P>  so I started dong that with my 5 speed saturn too. put it all the way up into 5th and let the car put along at 1500 on the straight and levels, then when I need to speed up or take on a hill - i mimick the TC lockup on the fiero and downshift.<P>   havent been driving the saturn enough to get a mpg rating yet (its been on the same tank of gas for the last month - Ive been taking the fiero all the time (c:<P>   My 85SE has a ECm disconnect right by the battery, on the + term lead - looks the same as the one on the O2 sensor - I pull that to reset my ecm.||||reg||1
